* Sun Sep 27 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.1-1
- Be inert on a machine that has no ITE8350, which is the promise the hw- prefix
  makes and which 1.0.0 did not keep. The resume check treated a missing
  accelerometer as a stale one, so on any other machine it reprobed a device that
  is not there, failed the unbind, and failed ite8350-resume-check.service about
  11 seconds after every resume, forever. Never dangerous -- the write names one
  device by its exact ACPI id, so nothing else's i2c-HID device could be unbound
  -- but wrong, and the sort of wrong a metapackage spreads.
- Reproduced before fixing and measured after, on a box with no such hub: 8
  seconds and exit 1 before, 0 seconds and exit 0 after.
- The precondition tests the I2C device node and not the accelerometer, which is
  the whole subtlety: unbinding the driver leaves the device in place, so the test
  is true exactly while the hardware is present and stays true in the wedged state
  the script exists to repair. Testing for accel_3d would have made the script
  exit 0 on precisely the failure it is for.
- Documentation= in both units pointed at file:///usr/share/doc/waydroid-bigtab01/
  docs/..., naming the superseded monolithic package and a path the doc macro flattens
  away, so both were dead links on an installed system.

* Sat Sep 26 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package, and the first waydroid-ext-hw-* one: docs/19's sensor-hub
  resume check, which reprobes i2c-ITE8350:00 when the accelerometer comes back
  from s2idle answering reads with a frozen value.
- Ships the two units, not the /etc/systemd/system-sleep/ite8350 hook they
  replaced. systemd 259 scans only /usr/lib/systemd/system-sleep, so that hook
  never ran once -- nine suspends, zero runs (docs/27). It is not in the source
  package either.
- Units go to /usr/lib/systemd/system rather than the installer's /etc default.
  A unit under /usr/local is labelled lib_t and init_t may not start it, which
  is exactly the path ite8350-sleep.service's ExecStop takes (docs/27).
- Depends on neither waydroid nor waydroid-ext-sensord. The payload only reads
  /sys/bus/iio and writes /sys/bus/i2c, so reviving the hub is worth doing
  whether or not Android is reading it, and nothing generic may depend on a
  package named after one laptop.
- Does not remove a pre-package manual install. /etc/systemd/system takes
  precedence over /usr/lib/systemd/system, so a host that still has the
  hand-installed units keeps running those and this package's are shadowed;
  remove them by hand before or after installing.
